 {"id": "EXAMPLE-APP-001", "title": "[P1] Prove the versatility thesis: ship ONE application built directly on the memory store (a task board), with no separate backend", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T17:51:08Z", "reasoning": "Bane's framing 2026-11-04: DuckBrain's point is VERSATILITY - 'how close can you run the database and application server' - so that a user or an agent builds the feature they want directly on the store instead of chasing a separate app backend. He points at a peer project (punkrecords) that ships a task manager and kanban board as proof of the idea. DuckBrain today has the PRIMITIVES and no DEMONSTRATION: 'duckbrain tables declare' writes a SUPA-6 schema.json, the generic REST layer serves CRUD with filters/order/limit plus PATCH/DELETE by primary key, per-table grants are enforced on every verb, the SUPA-2 serializer audits every mutation, X-Durability is on every write, and SSE publishes the change feed - all verified on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (declare widgets -> insert -> ?qty=gte. -> PATCH ?pk=eq. -> DELETE ?pk=eq. -> audit row with contentHash). What does not exist is a single shipped example an outsider can run to see that building an app on the store is real. Without one, the versatility claim is a thesis, not a product.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) A task-board application lives in the repo (e.g. examples/taskboard/) whose ENTIRE persistence is DuckBrain: tables declared in schema.json (e.g. boards, columns, cards with ids, ordering, timestamps), all reads through GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table with query params, all writes through POST/PATCH/DELETE including primary-key addressing. No second datastore, no SQLite, no JSON file of its own - prove it with 'grep -r' in the app dir showing zero alternate persistence. (2) It runs against a scratch daemon from a documented one-command start, and a committed transcript/screenshot shows: create board -> create column -> create card -> MOVE a card between columns (ordering change visible) -> filter cards by assignee -> delete a card -> the resulting rows read back correctly. (3) Permissions are exercised, not decorative: a token WITHOUT write grant can read the board but gets 403 on POST/PATCH/DELETE, and the denial appears in the namespace audit with reason table_grant. (4) A README section states in plain steps how a user declares their OWN tables and gets the same CRUD+grants+audit surface - the point is that the pattern generalises, not that this app exists. (5) The change feed is shown being used: subscribe to the namespace, move a card in another shell, and the event arrives. (6) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit as any new suite; full suite green before landing.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T19:08:55Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9c35865e60edca6b768cfd12c12fa8dbbdac84274df2bcb4e84e05ea94448360", "evidence": [{"run_id":"bc5cd660","ts":"2026-11-05T19:08:55Z"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-05T19:03:01Z; worktree wt/EXAMPLE-APP-001\n\nlanded 654b3c5b (ancestor of origin/main; examples/taskboard in its tree). AC1: grep -rniE 'localStorage|sessionStorage|indexedDB|sqlite|writeFile|appendFile|createWriteStream' over public/, serve.mjs, verify.mjs -> no output; serve.mjs only fs.readFile of static assets. AC2: docs/transcript.txt steps 1-6,10 (board->columns->cards, move via PATCH ?pk=, ordering change visible, assignee=eq. filter, DELETE, read-back). AC3: steps 7-8 reader token GET 200, POST/DELETE 403 FORBIDDEN, audit row {principal taskboard-reader, op tables.write, outcome denied, reason table_grant}. AC4: README 'Declare your own tables and get the same surface'. AC5: step 9 SSE event for a card moved by a second client, no polling. AC6: test-counts.json 256/2131 in the same commit; pnpm test:run 256 files/2131 tests green; tsc --noEmit clean; tier1 judge bc5cd660 PASS. UI verified in headless chrome: DOM renders 3 columns + 4 cards + healthy pill (docs/screenshot.png). Enabling fix: CORS middleware ran AFTER auth so a browser preflight got 401 with no ACAO — now CORS+preflight run before auth, X-API-Key/PATCH allowed, X-Durability/X-Total-Count exposed, pinned by src/cli/http-cors-example-app-001.test.ts (4/6 fail on pre-fix code). Second defect found and fixed here: replay.ts same-window insert+delete (row REPLAY-BIRTH-DEATH-001). NOT proven: no restart of the production daemon (:3000 untouched, scratch :28778 only); demo tokens are scratch keys exposed via /config.json (documented limit); no multi-user login flow."}
 {"id": "SEC-AUTHZ-001", "title": "[P1] 17 of 37 live API tokens are ADMIN because 'roles' is optional — the grant model is bypassed for half the fleet", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:06Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff while scoring our own tree (ours/duckbrain.json, row auth.legacy-token-admin-equivalence = WARNS_NEVER_REFUSES). src/auth/roles.ts:73-77: effectiveRoles() returns [\"admin\"] when principal.roles is undefined, documented as backward compatibility for the pre-SUPA-4 token shape. Measured against the live store (~/.duckbrain/auth.json, 37 keys): 17 have no roles field, and 37 of 37 carry no tableGrants. Consequence: (a) a token minted by any older tooling is a full admin on every namespace, silently — there is no signal at token creation and none at use; (b) the per-table grant layer SUPA-4 claims to enforce has never been exercised in production, so the only evidence it works is a scratch-daemon probe, not a deployment; (c) my own earlier report to the owner ('grants bite — verified: write-only grant GET 403, read-only DELETE 403') was true of the MECHANISM on a scratch daemon and misleading about the live system, which is the exact failure mode the boundary probe was supposed to prevent. Not a theoretical risk: an agent given a read-only key that was minted without a roles field can write and delete.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Make the default explicit and safe: a token with no roles field must not be admin — either default to a least-privilege role, or refuse to authenticate it with a clear error naming the token and the migration command. Prove with a test that a roles-less token cannot write to a namespace it was not granted, and that the failure names the reason. (2) Migrate the live population, do not just change the default: enumerate the 17 roles-less keys, assign each an intended role (ask the owner where intent is unknown — do NOT auto-grant admin to preserve the status quo), and re-count to 0 roles-less keys, printing before/after counts. (3) Exercise the grant layer for real at least once per role class: create one read-only token with explicit roles + explicit tableGrants and show GET 200 / PATCH 403 / DELETE 403 against a scratch daemon, with the denial visible in the namespace audit carrying reason table_grant. (4) Add a startup WARN (metrics + log) counting tokens without roles so the population cannot silently regrow; expose it as a number, not prose. (5) Update SUPA-4's row text: 'enforced' currently means 'the code can refuse', and it must also say how many live principals are actually subject to the rule.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:06Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "2ce285ea317189b9c9d714fbfa56626de1fd6547c5c9eca091fad2c802e5386c"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
 {"id": "DURABILITY-HONESTY-001", "title": "[P1] X-Durability reports the CONFIGURED mode, and the generic-table write path never fsyncs — the header overstates durability on the surface we sell as an app backend", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:07Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff (ours/duckbrain.json rows durability.x-durability-header-on-every-write = LOOKS_ENFORCED_IS_NOT and durability.fsync-before-ack-modes). src/storage/durability.ts:168-175: durabilityHeaderFor(ns, config) returns resolveWriteMode(ns, config) — a CONFIG LOOKUP, so the header states what the namespace is configured for, not what this write achieved. Separately, 'grep -rn fsync src/http/routes/tables.ts' returns nothing: the generic-table POST/PATCH/DELETE path acknowledges without any fsync, while the DDL coordinate path does fsync (src/serialization/ddl.ts:323-347, fsyncDir at :253). So a client that reads X-Durability: fsync from a table write is told something untrue on exactly the surface being pitched as an application backend. This also corrects my own report earlier today: I landed a row adding the header to table POST and described it as 'the durability header the write actually used' — it is the durability the namespace is CONFIGURED for.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Make the header truthful: either (a) have it report the mode the write actually achieved (the writer returns what it did, and a buffered write must never be able to claim fsync), or (b) rename/split it so configuration cannot be read as achievement (e.g. X-Durability-Mode: configured + a separate X-Durability-Achieved only where an fsync ran). Prove with a test that a namespace configured fsync but writing through a non-fsync path does NOT report an achieved fsync. (2) Close the gap it exposes: the generic-table write path must either fsync when the namespace is configured for it, or explicitly report buffered — and a test must fail if the achieved mode is weaker than the configured mode without that being visible to the client. (3) State the scope plainly in docs/specs/SUPA-1: which write paths honour fsync-before-ack today and which do not, path by path, with file:line. (4) Correct the earlier claim in the board record (the row that added the header to table POST) rather than leaving two contradictory statements — cite this row id in the correction.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T20:15:18Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "803a185a17be6175edc97f8f377cf95ac58a9033246e4f87045c9dd2f1fc4e03", "evidence": [{"run_id":"5ebe832b","ts":"2026-11-05T20:15:09Z"}]}, "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "47b1d5d01fa5b0ba93071e60846963f02d2b9dcf", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "SKIP", "worker_summary": "X-Durability reports the ACHIEVED mode; X-Durability-Configured + X-Durability-Degraded expose the gap; legacy table insert/PATCH/DELETE barrier via appendTextDurable/rewriteTextDurable. gitreins tier1 PASS 5ebe832b; suite 257 files / 2141 tests green; test-counts.json bumped.", "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-05T19:57:40Z; worktree wt/DURABILITY-HONESTY-001\n\nCLOSED 2026-11-05 by harden-loop, commit 47b1d5d0 (FF-landed on origin/main; verified ancestor of origin/main and the changed files are in that tree). AC1 header honesty: durabilityHeaders() unit tests prove configured/achieved separation and that a weaker achieved mode is named (configured=fsync; achieved=buffered); the literal case is test 'a non-fsync write path cannot report an achieved fsync for an fsync namespace' (appendRowsToTable mode=buffered on an fsync-configured ns returns achieved=buffered). AC2 generic-table barrier: src/http/routes/tables-durability-honesty.test.ts spies fs.fdatasyncSync per request - POST/PATCH/DELETE on an fsync namespace run >=1 barrier (buffered control = 0 syscalls); control probe with the barrier removed from appendTextDurable/rewriteTextDurable FAILED the 3 barrier tests, so they are load-bearing. AC3: docs/specs/SUPA-1-write-durability.md new section 'Write-path inventory' names every path with file:line (namespace-writer memory/declared-table paths achieve fsync/direct; legacy table insert+rewrite now fsync; audit-ledger tail, CLI memory-demote and search-index stay buffered BY DESIGN); docs/api/http-api.md and docs/specs/SUPA-3-rest.md updated. AC4: DB-SUPA-3 (the row that added X-Durability to table POST) carries an appended CORRECTION citing DURABILITY-HONESTY-001. NOT PROVEN / SCOPE: no probe against the live :3000 daemon (restart is owner-gated) - HTTP proofs ran on in-process scratch servers; 'direct' is unreachable for legacy table lines, such a write ACHIEVES fsync and reports the degradation instead of silently downgrading; audit tails/CLI tooling remain buffered (documented, not changed); a zero-row PATCH/DELETE reports the configured mode because no bytes are written."}
-{"id": "ADOPT-DECLARED-001", "title": "[P2] The declared-schema path is unused in production: 0 schema.json vs 949 legacy .table.json — and there is no HTTP/MCP way to declare a table", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:15Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff, and it is the counterpart of the versatility thesis: the mechanism exists and nobody uses it. Measured: find namespaces -maxdepth 2 -name schema.json = 0; find namespaces -name '*.table.json' = 949. So every SUPA-6 feature demonstrated today (declare -> CRUD -> filters -> PATCH/DELETE by pk -> audit) runs on a code path no namespace has adopted, while the legacy declaration shape serves every real table. Second half: declaration is host-CLI only (duckbrain tables declare, src/cli/tables.ts); src/cli/http.ts mounts no POST route for it and the MCP surface has no verb for it, so an AGENT cannot declare a table — it needs a shell on the box. That is precisely the 'build the feature you want directly on the store' claim blocked at step one: the store can serve arbitrary app tables, but only a human with SSH can create one.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Give declaration a network surface: POST /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table (declaration body) that writes through the same validated path as the CLI, with authz that requires a write/admin grant on that namespace, returning the resulting declaration; prove with an end-to-end run on a scratch daemon where an AGENT-equivalent HTTP client declares a table and immediately POSTs+GETs a row. Do not add a second implementation — the route must call the same coordinator the CLI uses. (2) Migration or an honest decision: either migrate a pilot namespace to schema.json (prove row-for-row equivalence: same table list, same primary key, same columns, same rows read back) or record why the legacy shape remains canonical and what legacy costs (e.g. no change records on those inserts, per DURABILITY-HONESTY-001/SUPA-2). (3) A startup or /metrics counter for 'namespaces with schema.json' vs 'with legacy declarations' so adoption is a number that moves. (4) Document the one path a user should take, in docs, replacing anything that implies schema.json is the default today. (5) Bump test-counts.json in the same commit as any new suite.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:15Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "02479c24ea983615c34d5f42b1cc80bf2bb3f9ea2fe960ccbc8908a3ea3931a9"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
+{"id": "ADOPT-DECLARED-001", "title": "[P2] The declared-schema path is unused in production: 0 schema.json vs 949 legacy .table.json — and there is no HTTP/MCP way to declare a table", "status": "in_progress", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:15Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff, and it is the counterpart of the versatility thesis: the mechanism exists and nobody uses it. Measured: find namespaces -maxdepth 2 -name schema.json = 0; find namespaces -name '*.table.json' = 949. So every SUPA-6 feature demonstrated today (declare -> CRUD -> filters -> PATCH/DELETE by pk -> audit) runs on a code path no namespace has adopted, while the legacy declaration shape serves every real table. Second half: declaration is host-CLI only (duckbrain tables declare, src/cli/tables.ts); src/cli/http.ts mounts no POST route for it and the MCP surface has no verb for it, so an AGENT cannot declare a table — it needs a shell on the box. That is precisely the 'build the feature you want directly on the store' claim blocked at step one: the store can serve arbitrary app tables, but only a human with SSH can create one.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Give declaration a network surface: POST /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table (declaration body) that writes through the same validated path as the CLI, with authz that requires a write/admin grant on that namespace, returning the resulting declaration; prove with an end-to-end run on a scratch daemon where an AGENT-equivalent HTTP client declares a table and immediately POSTs+GETs a row. Do not add a second implementation — the route must call the same coordinator the CLI uses. (2) Migration or an honest decision: either migrate a pilot namespace to schema.json (prove row-for-row equivalence: same table list, same primary key, same columns, same rows read back) or record why the legacy shape remains canonical and what legacy costs (e.g. no change records on those inserts, per DURABILITY-HONESTY-001/SUPA-2). (3) A startup or /metrics counter for 'namespaces with schema.json' vs 'with legacy declarations' so adoption is a number that moves. (4) Document the one path a user should take, in docs, replacing anything that implies schema.json is the default today. (5) Bump test-counts.json in the same commit as any new suite.", "updated_at": "2026-11-06T07:50:07Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "02479c24ea983615c34d5f42b1cc80bf2bb3f9ea2fe960ccbc8908a3ea3931a9"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-06T07:50:07Z; worktree wt/ADOPT-DECLARED-001"}
 {"id": "SURFACE-MCP-DATA-001", "title": "[P1] MCP reaches only memory primitives — no declared-table CRUD, no table declaration, no keyword search, no SQL query", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:36:35Z", "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-04/05 by enumerating all three surfaces rather than trusting the docs. MCP exposes 16 tools: remember, recall, forget, list_keys, list_namespaces, create_namespace, delete_namespace, squash, get_compaction_stats, server_status, server_http_start, switch_namespace, create_profile, update_profile, list_profiles, select_profiles. The generic-table layer - the thing this project calls its PostgREST-shaped app surface (SUPA-3) - has NO MCP tool at all: grep for table/CRUD tools in src/mcp/tools returns nothing but incidental mentions. Also absent: 'tables declare' (SUPA-6), keyword search (CLI 'search' + HTTP /api/search exist), and the read-only SQL 'query' verb. Consequence, and it is the versatility thesis failing at the surface that matters most: an agent connected over MCP can read and write memories but cannot declare a table, cannot insert a row into a declared table, cannot search by keyword, and cannot run a query - it must have a shell on the host for every one of those. Stdio/HTTP daemon both serve this same tool set, so this is not a transport gap.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Add MCP tools that call the SAME implementations the CLI and HTTP routes call (no second code path): tables_declare, table_insert, table_select (with the documented filter/select/order/limit vocabulary), table_update, table_delete, search, query. Each tool's schema must accept a namespace and reject an undeclared table with the same error text the HTTP layer uses. (2) Prove from a client, not from a unit test: drive tools/list and tools/call over stdio AND over /mcp, and paste: declare a table, insert 2 rows, select one with a filter, update it, delete it, keyword-search a memory, run one SELECT via query. (3) Grants must bite on the MCP path too: a token lacking table write must get a refusal from table_insert, and the denial must land in the namespace audit with reason table_grant — same as HTTP. (4) Bump test-counts.json in the same commit as any new suite; full suite green before landing.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T21:20:51Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "658c7c3e9dc60b75193f6b819100ca3c8464901ef35e029d994fb0af65ae1f98", "evidence": [{"run_id":"a0bac90b","ts":"2026-11-05T21:20:51Z"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": "1c2e3f0fa5cd4fe3fbea07b31a169616cd0e3156", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-05T21:05Z; worktree wt/SURFACE-MCP-DATA-001; tie-break with SURFACE-HTTP-OPS-001 (same updated_at) by lower blast radius — additive MCP surface, no live :3000 route change\n\n1c2e3f0f. AC1: table verbs moved to src/tables/operations.ts and declare to src/tables/declare.ts; REST handlers + 8 MCP tools (tables_declare, table_list, table_insert, table_select, table_update, table_delete, search, query) are thin adapters — no second code path; undeclared table returns the REST text+code (Table 'x' not found in namespace 'y' / NOT_FOUND) on both transports. AC2: real MCP SDK client over BOTH stdio and /mcp on scratch daemon :28734 (scratch namespaces path) declared, inserted 2, filtered select (qty=gte.6, count=exact), updated, deleted, keyword-searched and ran one SELECT — transcript docs/evidence/surface-mcp-data-001-2026-11-05.md, driver scripts/proofs/surface-mcp-data-001.mjs. AC3: token roles=[writer] tableGrants={widgets:read} refused table_insert with reason table_grant, message identical to requireTableGrant, denial landed in the namespace audit ledger (_audit/current.jsonl op=tables.write reason=table_grant); same token could read. AC4: test-counts.json 257/2141 -> 258/2148 in the same commit; full suite green (258 files / 2148 tests); gitreins judge a0bac90b tier1 PASS + tier2 PASS. NOT PROVEN: no rate-limit measurement on the new tools; the live :3000 daemon still runs the pre-change build until its next restart (tools appear after restart; no restart performed — owner-gated), and the new MCP HTTP tools were proven on the scratch daemon only."}
 {"id": "SURFACE-HTTP-OPS-001", "title": "[P1] HTTP serves data but no operations — squash, namespaces prune, s3, consolidate, distill-sessions, embeddings and token management are shell-only", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:36:35Z", "reasoning": "Enumerated 2026-11-05: src/cli/http.ts mounts no route for squash, prune, s3, consolidate, distill-sessions, embeddings or token management (grep for those route names returns nothing), while the CLI exposes every one of them as a verb. So the daemon can serve reads and app-shaped writes but cannot be asked to compact a namespace, prune a dead registry entry, run an S3 sync, produce the consolidation digest, distill sessions, rebuild the embedding cache, or mint/rotate a token - each of those needs a shell on the box. Two consequences: (a) the fleet's own lanes and any remote agent are second-class operators of this store; (b) the operational work that fixes the read-latency tail (squash) and the registry hygiene (prune) is exactly the work that cannot be triggered through the API, which is why it has been done by hand from a checkout.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) For EACH of squash, prune, s3 status/sync, consolidate, distill-sessions, embeddings rebuild, token create/revoke: either (a) expose it as a route with the same implementation the CLI calls, authorization requiring an admin grant, a dry-run default where the verb is destructive, and a response that reports what it did (counts, bytes, before/after); or (b) record a written decision in docs that it stays CLI-only, naming the security or safety reason, and add a test that asserts that decision so it cannot drift silently. Both outcomes are acceptable; silence is not. (2) For anything exposed, prove with a live scratch daemon and paste the transcript: squash a namespace and show the before/after file count, prune --dry-run over a seeded dead registry entry, s3 status, one token created then revoked and shown refused after revocation. (3) Destructive routes must refuse without an explicit confirm flag AND must be audited (op + outcome + principal) in the same ledger the other mutations use. (4) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit; full suite green before landing.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T22:42:40Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "f9edfcb6e00a92416cfd8cde1dab4af02e2f28fe12aedcfcd2c987dea5395155", "evidence": [{"run_id":"14d984d0","ts":"2026-11-05T22:42:40Z"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-05T22:08:16Z; worktree wt/SURFACE-HTTP-OPS-001\n\nlanded bd23bde6 (origin/main tip; src/http/routes/ops.ts + docs/api/ops-surface-decisions.md verified in its tree). EXPOSED, admin role + dry-run default + lifecycle-ledger line: POST /api/ops/namespaces/prune, GET /api/ops/s3/status, POST /api/ops/consolidate; POST /api/compaction/squash hardened in place (admin, dry-run by default, confirm:true required for a real run, one ledger line per run incl. refusals). CLI-ONLY DECISIONS with security/safety reasons + parity tests: s3 sync (bucket-wide blast radius, in-process lock for the sync's life, no progress/cancel channel), distill-sessions (reads host ~/.hermes/state.db, writes as the daemon principal), embeddings rebuild (paid + hours-long, no progress channel), token create/revoke (a stolen admin token would mint permanent admin credentials; revocation must not depend on the credential path). PROVEN on a live scratch daemon :28781 (own config/namespaces/auth): prune default left the registry sha256 unchanged (baaf80437f144655), dryRun:false -> 400 CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED, dryRun:false+confirm:true -> removed 1 mapping; s3 status enabled:false then enabled:true rows proof+seed (probe=false, no bucket touched); consolidate 2026-11-04 totals rows=3 unique=2 duplicates=1; squash default -> dryRun:true, dryRun:false -> 400, confirm:true -> partition squashed (current.jsonl replaced by data-*.parquet); writer token -> 403 on prune/s3-status/squash; CLI-only verbs -> 404. Suite 259 files / 2166 tests green; test-counts.json bumped in the landed commit. EVIDENCE RUN 14d984d0 = tier1 PASS (secrets + full suite), TIER2 SKIPPED: the first judge run hung 25 min with no child process at loadavg 27 and was killed, and two earlier tier1 reds were environmental (stale /home/kara/duckbrain/dist from Oct 6 missing the new MCP tools — rebuilt; then an ENOTEMPTY teardown race in http-cors-example-app-001 with all 2166 tests passing). NOT proven: a non-zero HISTORY compaction (scratch namespace had no partition older than 30 days) — the partition path was squashed instead, which is the file-count proof; s3 status remote counts were never probed against a real bucket."}
 {"id": "SURFACE-S3-001", "title": "[P2] S3 sync/query/config have no network surface — a remote agent cannot trigger a sync or see sync state without a shell", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:36:43Z", "reasoning": "The CLI ships 'duckbrain s3 status|sync|query|config' and the board records 17/17 S3 rows shipped, but src/cli/http.ts mounts no s3 route and the MCP tool set has no s3 tool, so an agent or a remote operator cannot answer 'is this namespace pushed?', 'sync it now', or 'query the object store' without SSH. This matters more than it looks: S3 is the durability/backup story for a store whose whole pitch is that it survives losing the box, and the one action that makes that true (sync) is the one an agent cannot take. It also blocks the compaction runbook: after compacting a namespace the S3 mirror holds ghost objects for every removed segment, and the cleanup step (s3 ghosts -> s3 clear -> s3 status) is shell-only.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Expose at minimum GET /api/ns/:ns/s3 (status: last push, object count, drift) and POST /api/ns/:ns/s3/sync (trigger), calling the same implementation the CLI uses, authorized as an admin/write grant on that namespace, returning the counts the CLI prints. (2) Prove on a scratch daemon with a local S3-compatible target (or the real bucket in a throwaway prefix): status before sync, sync, status after with the pushed count increasing, and a second sync proving idempotence (0 new objects). (3) Ghost cleanup from the compaction runbook must be reachable or explicitly documented as CLI-only with the reason; if reachable, POST .../s3/ghosts (list) + .../s3/clear (destructive, requires confirm, audited). (4) Add an MCP tool only if (1) lands cleanly — the deciding factor is whether an agent can complete the compaction runbook end to end without a shell; if it cannot, say so in the row rather than shipping half a surface. (5) test-counts.json bumped with any new suite.", "u