 {"id": "S3-PULL-INTEGRITY-001", "title": "[P1] s3 pull reports success while writing corrupted bytes — downloaded objects are never checksum-verified", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-06T00:09:33Z", "reasoning": "src/s3/sync.ts pullNamespace() catches per-object download failures with a console.warn only, and the delta decision for a fresh restore has no recorded etag/sha256 to compare against, so it downloads and trusts. Demonstrated 2026-11-05: an object overwritten with same-length garbage (hermes/event/2026-11/current.jsonl, 59,235 bytes) was reported as '1 succeeded, 0 failed' and served over HTTP; only the restore proof's own sha256 comparison caught it. For a store whose pitch is durability, a silent remote corruption becomes live memory on the next restore.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) REPRODUCE: corrupt one remote object with same-length garbage in a throwaway prefix, then run duckbrain s3 sync all pull into an empty root — today the engine prints '0 failed', writes the corrupt file, and GET /api/memories serves the corrupt content; transcript in docs/evidence/backup-restore-proof-2026-11-05.md (falsifier section). (2) FIX: verify every downloaded object before accepting it — a single-part ETag md5 when the listing provides one, else the sha256 recorded in our own manifest; on mismatch the run must be LOUD (nonzero exit, the object key named) and the file must not be left in place as if it had been restored. Also state what happens on a fresh machine where neither ETag nor manifest exists, rather than silently trusting the bytes. (3) PROVE with the control: scripts/restore-proof.sh --namespaces hermes --fault corrupt-one must now fail AT THE ENGINE (pull exits nonzero and names the key), and a clean run must stay green. (4) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit if the suite counts move.", "updated_at": "2026-11-06T02:38:11Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "f3bfc596d165ad8459c0e7b95991d43cbec8b71d790034409f0d96c8fc4d27d9", "evidence": [{"run_id":"301468f7","ts":"2026-11-06T02:38:11Z"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": "301468f7", "guard_result": "SKIP", "ci_result": "SKIP", "worker_summary": "Pull verifies each downloaded object before writing (ledger sha256, ETag md5, listing size, token-pinned manifest hash) and push publishes <prefix>/<ns>/.s3state/checksums.json so a fresh machine has an authority; the corrupt-one control now fails AT THE ENGINE.", "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-06T02:16:38Z; worktree wt/S3-PULL-INTEGRITY-001\n\nPROVEN: (1) reproduction = the pre-fix control transcript already in docs/evidence/backup-restore-proof-2026-11-05.md (engine said '0 failed', corrupt file restored); (2) fix = verifyDownloadedObject called BEFORE writeFileSync, ledger published by push, mismatch => key named + bytes never written + nonzero exit + namespace failure in 'sync all'; unreadable/absent ledger => unverified counted+warned, never silently trusted (fresh-machine statement in docs/s3-native.md 'Pull verifies what it restores' and evidence 'Fresh machine with neither ETag nor ledger'); (3) real-bucket control: scripts/restore-proof.sh --namespaces hermes --fault corrupt-one => '[S3] pull hermes/event/2026-11/current.jsonl FAILED (integrity): ledger-sha256 ... corrupt bytes NOT written', 'Sync complete: 1 attempted, 0 succeeded, 1 failed', engine exit 1, key named; clean run => downloaded=14 verified=14 unverified=0 failed=0, per-file sha256 PASS, exit 0; (4) test-counts.json 260/2174 -> 262/2194 in the same commit; full suite 262 files / 2194 tests green (env -u TMPDIR — a leaked TMPDIR under HOME makes the config-root tests resolve /home/kara/duckbrain.config.json). NOT PROVEN / NOT CLAIMED: --fault delete-one is still caught only by the proof's own hash comparison (pull cannot verify bytes that never arrive; ledger-completeness gap filed as S3-PULL-COMPLETENESS-001); the ledger is unsigned, so this detects corruption, not a hostile writer who can rewrite both object and ledger; ledger cost measured only on hermes (15 objects), scheduler's 258k files would publish a ~25MB ledger. Note: src/s3/client-timeout-s3git006 fails intermittently in this sandbox (reproduced 2 failed on unmodified origin/main 0deb8fa0) — pre-existing, not from this change."}
 {"id": "S3-PULL-COMPLETENESS-001", "title": "[P2] s3 pull cannot detect an object that is MISSING remotely although the checksum ledger lists it", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-06T02:38:26Z", "reasoning": "Found while landing S3-PULL-INTEGRITY-001 (301468f7). Verification now covers bytes that ARRIVE; it says nothing about objects that never arrive. scripts/restore-proof.sh --fault delete-one removes one remote object and the run is still only caught by the proof's own file-count/sha256 comparison — the engine reports success, because pull enumerates the REMOTE listing and a deleted object simply produces no download to verify. The ledger (published by the push) already knows every path that should exist, so the engine has what it needs to detect this itself.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) REPRODUCE: scripts/restore-proof.sh --namespaces hermes --fault delete-one on the current tree — transcript shows the engine exiting 0 while the file is missing (proof catches it at step 6). (2) FIX: when a namespace's checksum ledger is present, a path listed in the ledger with no object in the remote listing is a FAILURE — nonzero exit naming the path, no local file touched or deleted — reported through the same SyncFailure channel (kind missing) that S3-PULL-INTEGRITY-001 added, and counted in 'sync all' as a namespace failure. State explicitly what happens when no ledger exists (nothing to compare — the pull cannot claim completeness). (3) PROVE: delete-one now fails AT THE ENGINE (key named, exit nonzero) and the clean run stays green; (4) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit if counts move.", "updated_at": "2026-11-06T02:38:26Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "4f83eb05f1dd7eeb611f9afcd385822c30da46107711225f302976baea604337"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
+{"id": "DECLARE-MIGRATE-001", "title": "[P2] Migrate the 949 legacy table declarations to schema.json (owner-gated: rewrites live namespace declarations)", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-06T07:54:56Z", "reasoning": "Filed by ADOPT-DECLARED-001 (2026-11-06), which gave declaration a network surface and measured the split on the live store: 0 schema.json vs 949 tables/*.table.json across 71 of 297 namespace directories. Legacy declarations win on name conflict (src/schema/table-registry.ts), insert through the direct-append branch of insertTableRows (no SUPA-2 serializer, no change record), and carry no declared relations or composite keys. Adoption is now a number on /metrics, so this row's progress is measurable rather than argued. Live-namespace mutation was explicitly out of scope for ADOPT-DECLARED-001 (hard rule: never rewrite production namespaces).", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Per namespace, per table: declare the SAME contract in schema.json over POST /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table/declare (no legacy flag), prove row-for-row parity (GET rows + X-Total-Count identical to the saved pre-change response), THEN delete namespaces/<ns>/tables/<table>.table.json and prove parity again plus one insert that lands through the NamespaceWriter (DB-SUPA-5 change record present, which the legacy direct append never wrote). (2) Rollback per table = restore the deleted legacy file (legacy wins on conflict). (3) Re-count to zero divergences: find namespaces -name '*.table.json' | wc -l == 0, and /metrics duckbrain_table_declaration_namespaces{format=legacy} == 0 while {format=schema.json} equals the number of namespaces that held declarations (71 on 2026-11-06). (4) Do it in batches of at most 5 namespaces with a saved before/after response per table, never a bulk rewrite, and state the batching in the row.", "updated_at": "2026-11-06T07:54:56Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "0636d9e206496c43a005deebd737bb9367b47cdbc2b54aff7e51b030ba678f72"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null, "blocked_reason": "OWNER GATE: the migration deletes tables/<table>.table.json files inside PRODUCTION namespaces (/home/kara/duckbrain/namespaces). The hardening loop may not rewrite live memory; recipe + parity gates: docs/decisions/ADOPT-DECLARED-001-declaration-shape.md."}
